# Git Fetch & Merge — MANDATORY Protocol

## The Problem

`git fetch origin <branch>` writes to `FETCH_HEAD` only — it does NOT update `refs/remotes/origin/<branch>`. Merging against the remote-tracking ref after such a fetch uses stale data and silently reports "Already up to date" even when new commits exist.

## MANDATORY: Use Explicit Refspec for All Fetch Operations

### When fetching a specific branch:

```bash
# ✅ CORRECT — Always use explicit refspec to update remote-tracking ref
git fetch origin refs/heads/<branch>:refs/remotes/origin/<branch>

# ✅ ALSO CORRECT — Pull directly (fetches + merges from FETCH_HEAD)
git pull origin <branch>

# ❌ WRONG — Only updates FETCH_HEAD, NOT origin/<branch>
git fetch origin <branch>
```

### When fetching all branches:

```bash
# ✅ CORRECT — Fetches all refs using configured refspecs
git fetch origin --prune

# ✅ CORRECT — Force-update all remote-tracking refs
git fetch origin '+refs/heads/*:refs/remotes/origin/*' --prune
```

## MANDATORY: Verify After Every Fetch

After every fetch, ALWAYS verify the remote-tracking ref was actually updated before merging:

```bash
# Step 1: Check what the remote actually has
git ls-remote origin <branch>

# Step 2: Check what the local tracking ref has
git rev-parse origin/<branch>

# Step 3: If they differ, the fetch didn't update the ref — re-fetch with explicit refspec
```

## MANDATORY: Never Trust "Already up to date"

If `git merge origin/<branch>` says "Already up to date" after a fetch intended to pull new code:

1. **Do NOT accept it at face value**
2. Run `git ls-remote origin <branch>` to check the actual remote HEAD
3. Compare with `git rev-parse origin/<branch>` locally
4. If they differ, re-fetch with explicit refspec:
   ```bash
   git fetch origin refs/heads/<branch>:refs/remotes/origin/<branch>
   ```
5. Then retry the merge

## Complete Pull Workflow (Use This Every Time)

```bash
# 1. Fetch with explicit refspec
git fetch origin refs/heads/<branch>:refs/remotes/origin/<branch>

# 2. Verify the ref was updated
git log --oneline -5 origin/<branch>

# 3. Check what's incoming
git log --oneline HEAD..origin/<branch>

# 4. Merge
git merge origin/<branch>
```

## Stash Protocol (When Local Changes Block Merge)

```bash
# 1. Stash local changes
git stash push -m "Stash before merging <branch>"

# 2. Perform merge (using workflow above)
git merge origin/<branch>

# 3. Restore local changes
git stash pop

# 4. If stash pop conflicts, resolve them
```
